cheduledAt.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)` — a non-secret timestamp, safe to record verbatim), append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the delete path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag + telemetry.FromContext for correlation; returns 202 Accepted (the scheduling is durable but the destructive teardown is a later worker job). The wire DTO (projectEnvironment / projectResource) gains a `DeletionScheduledAt *string \`json:"deletion_scheduled_at,omitempty"\`` field so a live row's JSON shape is byte-identical to before (omitempty + nil pointer = field absent), while a scheduled row exposes the stamp as RFC 3339 — mirror this on every resource DTO that gains a soft-delete column. Routes.go registration uses `Method: http.MethodDelete`, `SuccessStatus: http.StatusAccepted`, the same `environmentIDResolver` as GET/PATCH (the bare-id three routes share one resolver), and the same description shape as `deleteProject`'s scheduled-not-immediate / version-pre-check / one-tx-audit clauses. NewHandler+newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (`environmentDeleter EnvironmentDeleter` at the very tail of the env ports group, after `environmentUpdater`); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, nil)` -> `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` and `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, logger)` (37 NewHandler call sites across 37 files) handled the bulk; the named-updater outlier `fakeEnvironmentReader{}, updater, nil)` in the resource's own update test got `..., updater, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)`; the all-nil newRouteTable form (7 files) received one trailing nil; the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form (projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go 