 - PATCH endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0157 pattern, PATCH /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.Update + classifyEnvironmentConcurrencyMiss, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Update + buildEnvironmentUpdate, httpapi/environments.go updateEnvironmentHandler): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource PATCH endpoints. Repository.Update takes a fully-formed row + *int64 ifMatchVersion, runs a tenant-scoped (organization_id, id) UPDATE that touches only the mutable columns (slug, display_name — NEVER the composite tenant key), and classifies a zero-row return via a classify*ConcurrencyMiss helper that disambiguates gone-vs-stale by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator runs the validation BEFORE the transaction is opened (buildXxxUpdate is split out for unit-testability, rejects empty patches as 400, never echoes submitted values in violations), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale with row's authoritative version BEFORE the version-checked UPDATE — even a trivial no-op patch with stale If-Match surfaces the conflict), apply patch in-memory, call Repository.Update, append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the update path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses the shared parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag helpers (yes, the helper is org-named but it is the SHARED chokepoint for every endpoint mirroring versions to ETag — do not duplicate it per resource family). Body is strictly decoded with `slug *string` and `display_name *string` (pointer nil-ness IS the partial-update contract); the body intentionally exposes no organization_id/parent_id/self_id field — tenant isolation is structural. Response is yalla.output.v1 carrying the same wire shape GET returns, plus ETag header. Path with NO parent_id (bare-id route): the resource scope pins only OrganizationID + the deep id, so the engine's covers() rule DENIES every scoped grant at the policy boundary (a grant with a pinned ProjectID cannot cover a resource with no ProjectID); principals whose only access is a scoped grant must use a parent-scoped route. Every NewHandler positional-arg addition triggers a ~50-site sweep across ~35 existing test files — anchor on `fakeXxxReader{}, nil)` and `fakeXxxReader{}, logger)` plus the `reader, nil)` / `reader, logger)` outliers in the resource's own get/policy test helpers. The newRouteTable-only OpenAPI shape tests (all-nils form, partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form) each need one more `nil` appended. AVOID double-counting: run go vet AFTER each automated sweep and undo overshoots before continuing.
 - DELETE endpoint for a deep resource by bare id (BE-0160 pattern, DELETE /v1/environments/{environment_id}, migration 0016_environment_deletion_schedule, store/environment.go EnvironmentRepository.ScheduleDeletion, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.ScheduleDeletion + DeleteEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go deleteEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentDeleter): canonical shape for ALL deep-resource SOFT-delete endpoints. The migration is structurally identical to 0013_project_deletion_schedule — `ALTER TABLE <resource> ADD COLUMN deletion_scheduled_at timestamptz` + a partial index `WHERE deletion_scheduled_at IS NOT NULL` for the future teardown-worker scan. The repository ScheduleDeletion is an SQL-IDEMPOTENT unconditional UPDATE stamping `deletion_scheduled_at = now()` returning the row in column order (so callers see the trigger-refreshed updated_at + bumped version); the already-scheduled distinction is the service's job (the repository must remain SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers — workers, admin jobs — that retry). ifMatchVersion uses the SAME pattern as Update — nil disables, non-nil adds `AND version = $N` to the WHERE — and classify*ConcurrencyMiss disambiguates the no-rows case (gone vs stale) by Get-in-same-tx. The service-layer orchestrator validates blank-input BEFORE opening the tx (typed InvalidInput, missing actor org is Internal — wiring error, never client input), then in ONE Store.Write: read current row, check If-Match pre-condition (stale -> ConflictStale BEFORE the already-scheduled check, so a stale caller learns version-stale instead of an already-scheduled message that races with concurrent edits they did not see), reject already-scheduled as apierr.Conflict (rolls the whole tx back so the audit record can never name a deletion that did not change resource state), call Repository.ScheduleDeletion, record the resolved deletion_scheduled_at stamp as audit metadata (`event.Metadata["deletion_scheduled_at"] = row.DeletionScheduledAt.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)` — a non-secret timestamp, safe to record verbatim), append audit row. UNLIKE Create, the orchestrator does NOT invoke the in-tx Authorizer or QuotaReserver — the HTTP RequireAuth gate is authoritative for the delete path; the in-tx Authorize is reserved for Create's defence-in-depth against grant-change races during quota reservation. The HTTP handler reuses parseIfMatchVersion + writeOrganizationETag + telemetry.FromContext for correlation; returns 202 Accepted (the scheduling is durable but the destructive teardown is a later worker job). The wire DTO (projectEnvironment / projectResource) gains a `DeletionScheduledAt *string \`json:"deletion_scheduled_at,omitempty"\`` field so a live row's JSON shape is byte-identical to before (omitempty + nil pointer = field absent), while a scheduled row exposes the stamp as RFC 3339 — mirror this on every resource DTO that gains a soft-delete column. Routes.go registration uses `Method: http.MethodDelete`, `SuccessStatus: http.StatusAccepted`, the same `environmentIDResolver` as GET/PATCH (the bare-id three routes share one resolver), and the same description shape as `deleteProject`'s scheduled-not-immediate / version-pre-check / one-tx-audit clauses. NewHandler+newRouteTable signatures each grow ONE positional port (`environmentDeleter EnvironmentDeleter` at the very tail of the env ports group, after `environmentUpdater`); a Python regex sweep over the test directory anchored on `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, nil)` -> `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` and `fakeEnvironmentUpdater{}, logger)` (37 NewHandler call sites across 37 files) handled the bulk; the named-updater outlier `fakeEnvironmentReader{}, updater, nil)` in the resource's own update test got `..., updater, fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)`; the all-nil newRouteTable form (7 files) received one trailing nil; the partial-fakes-with-trailing-nils form (projects_delete_test.go + projects_restore_test.go 