 - New top-level resource family that already has its persistence table from an earlier migration (BE-0148 pattern, environments table from 0002 + version column from 0011, store/environment.go, httpapi/project_environments.go): a reader-only scoped-child vertical lands JUST the GET endpoint and pairs with the existing table — NO new migration. The vertical is structurally identical to BE-0142 (project_variables reader) with one narrowing worth pinning: when the table already exists (environments was created by 0002_tenant_hierarchy.up.sql at the same time as projects/services, and gained its version + bump_version trigger in 0011_optimistic_versions.up.sql), the story collapses into store layer + http layer + wiring + tests — no migration to declare composite FKs, scope-uniqueness indexes, or triggers. The store layer (Environment struct, EnvironmentRepository.ListByProject, EnvironmentReader.ListProjectEnvironments) mirrors ProjectVariableRepository/ProjectVariableReader exactly — every read is tenant-scoped at the SQL predicate, the reader Gets the project first inside the same Store.Read tx so a cross-tenant or unknown project_id surfaces as apierr.NotFound (never an empty list), and the repository has its own list cap (environmentListMaxRows = 500). Crucially, environments carry NO credential material (the table stores only id, organization_id, project_id, slug, display_name, version, created_at, updated_at) so the HTTP projection (projectEnvironmentOf) needs no value-redaction chokepoint — every field is structural — but the projection STILL exists as a single chokepoint so a future column added to store.Environment is reviewed for its wire exposure here rather than leaking by default. The integration tests (store/environment_test.go) prove ordering, tenant scoping (a cross-tenant (orgA, projB_id) pair yields no rows), the empty-project shape (non-nil empty slice), the reader's project existence check, and that a cross-tenant id surfaces as apierr.NotFound WIT